        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Can I keep using a treadmill with a cracked deck?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. A cracked deck can flex more with each step and eventually break under a footstrike, and a raised edge can damage the belt and load the motor. Unplug the treadmill, remove the safety key so nobody else uses it, and get the deck inspected and replaced before using the machine again.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you flip a cracked treadmill deck?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. Flipping only helps when the old surface is worn but the board is sound. A crack runs through the board, so the other face sits on the same broken structure and will not stay flat. A cracked deck needs replacing, usually together with the belt.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can a cracked treadmill deck be glued or repaired?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It is not a reliable repair. A glued crack rarely stays flush under repeated impact, a brace screwed underneath can foul the frame or belt, and screws can rise into the belt. Because the deck carries a person on a moving belt, replacement is the safe fix. Cushions, bolts, rails and belts around the deck can often be repaired.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much does it cost to replace a cracked treadmill deck?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              As typical estimates, a replacement deck commonly costs in the region of $100 to $300 as a part for a home treadmill, and a technician fitting a deck and belt together commonly comes to $300 to $600 or more all in. Premium and commercial machines cost more. Check the warranty before paying.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What causes a treadmill deck to crack?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Common causes are a user above the weight limit, a board already softened by surface wear or moisture, impacts from dropped weights or a dropped folding deck, damage during a move, missing or split cushions under the deck, and over-tightened deck bolts. Occasionally it is a manufacturing defect, which a warranty may cover.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a cracked treadmill deck covered under warranty?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It can be. Decks are often covered for shorter terms than frames or motors, but an early crack on a machine used within its weight limit may be a defect, and a crack in the steel frame falls under the frame warranty. Contact the manufacturer with photographs, the serial number and proof of purchase before ordering parts.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

